WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.750
Hello everybody, this is Peter Cooper from
NCBI.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.940
Welcome to today's webinar on how to quickly
retrieve sequences from NCBI.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:14.550
During the webinar if you have a question
please type it in the question pod.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:19.150
My colleague Tao is here and he will try to
answer them in real time while we go through

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:20.560
the webinar.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:25.000
If there are some particularly relevant ones
for everyone, we will take time at the end

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.930
to try to answer those.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.840
For questions that we have, we will put the
answers in the document that will be available

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.100
after the webinar, linked to the webinar page.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.320
I will go ahead and start the webinar.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:43.600
We will talk today in sort of basic terms
about how to retrieve sequences from NCBI.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.320
My name is Peter Cooper as I said.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.080
You can write to me about any questions at
Peter dot Cooper at NCBI.gov.

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.000
I'll show you some other contact addresses
at the end of this.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.540
We're basically going to learn how to display
and download sequences, using the NCBI webpages

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:04.410
. Something a lot of people are are not aware
how to do.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:09.400
And how to bookmark sequences and things like
that using direct URLs.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:17.400
Then we will switch over to the command line,
using Unix and Linux and the E-Utilities,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:25.030
very briefly and then switch over to the EDirect
programs which is a simpler way of doing that.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:27.909
We're going to talk first about accessing
sequences on the web and I'll show you a few

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:32.500
slides and then we will go live.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.380
When you're downloading sequences are accessing
sequences in NCBI you will search for them

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.110
in the Entrez system.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.810
Most people are aware of this system, they
may not be aware of the name.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:46.670
We have gotten away from calling it that but
that is the way we call it here at NCBI.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:54.700
We're going to use two main sequence databases
today, we will use, nucleotide or nuccore.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.850
And the same things that I show you also apply
to the protein databases.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:06.680
We will talk about the nucleotide but the
same applies to the protein sequences as well.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.849
If you go to the Entrez system there are some
display formats, we will run a search and

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:13.389
get some sequences.

00:02:13.389 --> 00:02:19.999
You see by default the Summary format, you
can get these various displays, GenBank, FASTA.

00:02:19.999 --> 00:02:24.260
Those are the two I will emphasize.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:28.959
THose are the URLs on the right side of the
slide to those web pages.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:34.989
It's just the NCBI base URL (www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov)
and the datagbase name (/nucleotide or /protein).

00:02:34.989 --> 00:02:40.040
We'll come back in a minute when we talk about
bookmarking sequences.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:47.180
So the two common formats that people run
into the most at our site are Genbank and

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:48.180
FASTA.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:55.719
GenBank is human readable, it has a header
it has a sequence annotation, and the sequence

00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:57.370
itself.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:02.029
And it's the same format that is used on the
GenBank FTP.

00:03:02.029 --> 00:03:06.999
The sequence we're going to be looking at
is a Reference Sequence, but the format is

00:03:06.999 --> 00:03:08.579
still the GenBank format.

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:13.949
And then there is fasta, what most people
probably want when they're coming to get a

00:03:13.949 --> 00:03:15.090
sequence.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:16.620
It is a real simple format.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:22.709
It has the title on one line with a greater
than sign that starts the title, and it has

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:25.180
a sequence on the subsequent lines.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:31.870
FASTA, basically a universal format for sequence
analysis programs . We have a text version

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:36.790
of it, where you can copy and paste easily
even if you have multiple sequences.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.930
One thing you notice about these is you can
not download these directly from the web browser

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.610
using the browser's file menu.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:49.169
The way you get around that is use the Send
to menu.

00:03:49.169 --> 00:03:56.029
In the upper right-hand corner of the displayed
pages.

00:03:56.029 --> 00:04:01.609
If I click on that, it opens up and gives
me a bunch of radio buttons I can choose.

00:04:01.609 --> 00:04:05.799
If I want to download a complete record you
can do that.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:09.760
We'll come back to this later you can also
get the coding sequences or the gene features.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.510
Things annotated on the record.

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:17.690
We choose the file, the destination, then
press that Create File button and download

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.640
in text format.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:24.470
The options here, the three actually that
I'm going to talk about, are GenBank which

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.470
we already mentioned.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:32.410
GenBank full, a special format that you will
have to use on large sequence records like

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:39.070
chromosome records because they will not have
the sequence in them.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.770
And then FASTA which is the most important
for downloading.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:46.940
And then there are other structured formats
we won't go into today . Also identifier lists.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.680
We will focus on getting sequences.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:54.200
Another topic I want to mention, the ability
to bookmark sequences.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:00.750
There is a standard way you can link to a
record in the Entrez system.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:06.880
NCBI base URL, database name, and the id that
is at the bottom of the slide . For example

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.570
if I want to link directly to a nucleotide
sequence, this one at the top, www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nuccore/NM_000485.2

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:17.880
It is the base URL, the name of the database,
you can put nucleotide or nuccore here, then

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.260
the identifier which is the accession number.

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:26.150
And likewise I can add this feature at the
end, question mark report equals fasta, and

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:31.050
and you get the fasta.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:36.320
And then finally you can link to the text
record by adding ampersand format equals text.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.960
You can do this with more than one record,
comma separated.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.620
Okay, that is all well and good.

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.350
But mostly that's for displaying things.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:49.460
What we want to do is access sequences directly
without using a web browser, to get them locally

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.540
for us to use.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:58.550
The standard way to do that is the E-Utilities
. This is NCBI's API.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:02.090
It uses URLs to access the system.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.510
These can be incorporated into scripts.

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:09.660
Basically there's a base URL, and a bunch
of server-side programs you run.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:15.630
They do require that you write a custom wrapper
script to integrate search and retrieval.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:20.610
An efetch example, which would basically do
the same thing we just saw on the bookmark

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.180
links, is listed at the bottom of the slide.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.180
https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/efetch.fcgi?db=nucleotide&amp;id=NM_000485.2&amp;rettype=fasta

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.510
You have the base URL here.

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:28.940
It's kind of long.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.540
And then you have the CGI program efetch.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:40.630
The nucleotide databae, the id you want, and
the return type, in this case FASTA . That

00:06:40.630 --> 00:06:47.110
gives you a FASTA formatted record for this
particular NCBI reference sequence.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:50.980
That is fine if you're comfortable writing
scripts.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:56.670
There is a set of scripts that NCBI has made
called Entrez direct . Basically a commandline

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.090
version of the EUtilities.

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:05.440
These programs run on UNIX or Linux operating
system.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.230
You can basically use them to do things like
sequence search and retrieval.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:13.550
We will focus mainly today and retrieval.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:18.960
Which means we will use this program called
efetch . There's a lot more to Entrez Direct

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.090
but we won't go into it today .

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:23.230
Why do you want to use this?

00:07:23.230 --> 00:07:29.930
It lets you get the sequences directly with
batch access without a web browser and most

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:33.180
importantly there is no scripting required.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:36.230
So this is a slide about EDirect.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:45.060
We're going to be looking at that center section,
We will basically perform a search and then

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.920
use efetch to download this.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:53.230
There's a lot more to it, you have a parser
in there if you get the xml format.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:58.820
If you want to know more about how to use
EDirect, we did an one hour webinar on this

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.440
and you can watch the recording on our YouTube
channel.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.000
Follow this link.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.000
https://youtu.be/BLnYW33Mtb0

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.139
How these programs work?

00:08:05.139 --> 00:08:09.900
They work in a very similar way to the way
Entrez works so you run a search using the

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.040
esearch program.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:15.960
And this will put some things in the Entrez
history which you don't need to worry about.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:24.781
Then I use the UNIX pipe and then put it into
this program efetch , and then I will get

00:08:24.781 --> 00:08:28.670
some format, in this case I just asked for
the accession numbers.

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:30.270
$ esearch -db nucleotide -query "human[organism]
AND APRT[title] AND refseq[filter] | efetch

00:08:30.270 --> 00:08:31.270
-format acc

00:08:31.270 --> 00:08:35.680
For the sequence databases there are lots
of formats available in Entrez Direct.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.639
Many of these you see on the pulldown list
on the web.

00:08:41.639 --> 00:08:47.079
There are lots of additional ones, some additional
ones here because you have an XML format,

00:08:47.079 --> 00:08:49.560
text format, for example for the GenBank flat
file.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.610
When you're working with these you will see
if you want to know what the options are type

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:57.550
the name of the program then dash help.

00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:02.100
In order to use these you have to install
them and the information on how to do that

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.670
I won't go over today.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.260
It's very straightforward.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:10.821
If you go to the Entrez Direct help documents
that I will point out in a little bit, you

00:09:10.821 --> 00:09:15.550
should be able to install them simply on any
UNIX type system.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:22.279
For more information about E direct a really
good place to go for recipes for how do you

00:09:22.279 --> 00:09:30.589
do certain things is this cookbook available
on the NCBI Hackathons GitHub site.

00:09:30.589 --> 00:09:33.420
The URL is in the center of this slide.

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:34.420
https://github.com/NCBI-Hackathons/EDirect_EUtils_API_Cookbook

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:39.670
Okay, what I want to do now are live demonstrations
of how to manage these kinds of things on

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.460
the web and then we'll work on the command
line.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.040
I will escape from the sides.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.540
And I will go over here to the web browser.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:51.050
And I am going to go through some examples.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:54.370
Let's work with a particular set of genes
and gene products.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:58.190
What I will do first is get human sequences.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:01.450
I will do an organism fielded search.

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:07.920
And I will type the name of the gene APRT,
which is adenine phosphoribosyl transferase.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.449
We will search the nucleotide database.

00:10:10.449 --> 00:10:18.050
You can select it from this pulldown list
or Google it directly.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:24.070
So I already had my RefSeq filter on, I can
take that off, you can see the difference

00:10:24.070 --> 00:10:26.010
if you don't have that on.

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:31.829
So I get 22 but I will reduce it down to seven.

00:10:31.829 --> 00:10:35.050
And so, this is interesting to notice what
kind of records we have.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:40.110
We have transcript variants from APRT.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:44.449
We have this RefSeqGene record which is a
standalone record which represents that genomic

00:10:44.449 --> 00:10:48.350
region plus some sequence on either side of
the gene.

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:53.040
We also have some additional RefSeqGene records
because the flanking sequence contains parts

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.160
of these genes here, also on chromosome 16.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:02.910
And we have the entire chromosome 16 record
here, two versions of it, one from the primary

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:06.339
assembly and one from the CHM one assembly.

00:11:06.339 --> 00:11:14.720
I will work with the first three sequences.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:21.769
I don't want to deal with downloading that
human chromosome.

00:11:21.769 --> 00:11:25.190
In the pulldown list I can change the format
easily.

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:29.160
We can change it to GenBank .

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:33.519
And we get all of the records here in GenBank
format, human readable, with lots of information

00:11:33.519 --> 00:11:38.209
on the annotation on the records.

00:11:38.209 --> 00:11:39.550
Or we can choose FASTA .

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:46.200
And there are the three records in FASTA format.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:50.069
What a lot of people do, they select these.

00:11:50.069 --> 00:11:55.639
You select them one at a time and copy and
paste them into an text file or sometimes

00:11:55.639 --> 00:11:58.189
they paste them into the BLAST tool.

00:11:58.189 --> 00:12:01.209
Notice you don't need to do that by the way.

00:12:01.209 --> 00:12:05.750
You can get a set of sequences on the webpage
here and click the Run BLAST button and it

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:12.600
will take the accessions and put them in the
BLAST form, you're ready to go.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.050
This is still a little bit inconvenient so
there's another more convenient way to get

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.869
these if you want to select all three sequence
at once.

00:12:18.869 --> 00:12:25.670
There is a FASTA (text) version here.

00:12:25.670 --> 00:12:27.589
There they are.

00:12:27.589 --> 00:12:34.899
If I select and copy and paste these, I will
get these in text format.

00:12:34.899 --> 00:12:38.110
In a way that is convenient, but it's not
convenient if I was trying to do this with

00:12:38.110 --> 00:12:39.339
hundreds of sequences.

00:12:39.339 --> 00:12:44.910
So in order to do that we use the Send to
menu on the right-hand side.

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:48.449
Of course as I pointed out if you wanted to
save these with the web browser function,

00:12:48.449 --> 00:12:53.389
you just get a web page with all the HTML
stuff and you don't want that.

00:12:53.389 --> 00:12:56.249
You can use the send to menu here.

00:12:56.249 --> 00:12:58.970
Choose file as the destination.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:03.850
And then quickly download the FASTA format
, you saw what that looks like.

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:08.949
You can also download the GenBank format and
if I was dealing with that chromosome 16 record

00:13:08.949 --> 00:13:11.519
I would use the GenBank full format.

00:13:11.519 --> 00:13:15.999
There's a bunch of other formats here that
we won't go into today . basically structured

00:13:15.999 --> 00:13:18.029
formats of various kinds.

00:13:18.029 --> 00:13:20.480
Also an identifier list.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:27.170
And relatively recently the GFF, another way
of looking at the annotation.

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:32.709
One useful thing here is to get the coding
sequences, just to show you how that works.

00:13:32.709 --> 00:13:35.089
Let me go back and grab one of these really
quick.

00:13:35.089 --> 00:13:37.949
I will retrieve this sequence.

00:13:37.949 --> 00:13:44.829
This is the transcript variant one.

00:13:44.829 --> 00:13:48.069
Notice there is a feature called the CDS.

00:13:48.069 --> 00:13:49.170
The CDS I can highlight.

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:53.999
It starts with a start codon and ends with
the stop codon.

00:13:53.999 --> 00:14:01.679
So this is part of this messenger RNA sequence
that is translated into protein.

00:14:01.679 --> 00:14:05.670
We can get those for all of the records in
the list if we want to.

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:11.470
For things like messenger RNAs it will be
nice and simple, one coding region.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:17.079
For the gene record we will have several of
them corresponding to perhaps different transcript

00:14:17.079 --> 00:14:20.519
splice variants that are annotated on the
gene record.

00:14:20.519 --> 00:14:24.989
And also the fact this particular record will
have parts of other genes besides APRT.

00:14:24.989 --> 00:14:29.499
So just to show you a download format, I will
do Send to.

00:14:29.499 --> 00:14:33.260
I will send the coding sequences.

00:14:33.260 --> 00:14:38.509
And I will download FASTA nucleotide . I will
put that on my desktop.

00:14:38.509 --> 00:14:42.569
I will call it CDS.txt.

00:14:42.569 --> 00:14:55.819
And I will go back here and I'm going to drop
it into this.

00:14:55.819 --> 00:15:00.589
You can see, you get these nice coding regions
with very nice structured deflines, telling

00:15:00.589 --> 00:15:10.139
you what gene it is, where it is and those
types of things.

00:15:10.139 --> 00:15:15.779
You can also, see the various coding regions
on this RefSeqGene record.

00:15:15.779 --> 00:15:18.399
These are very handy for what coding regions
are on there.

00:15:18.399 --> 00:15:20.660
In some cases some of these are partial.

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:26.360
And then there's a complete one, of course
for the APRT gene.

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:32.509
Okay, so, we already talked sufficiently in
the slides about how to bookmark the sequences,

00:15:32.509 --> 00:15:34.829
you can easily follow the instructions in
the slides.

00:15:34.829 --> 00:15:41.779
What I want to do now is go to the command
line, to show you a couple of things there.

00:15:41.779 --> 00:15:47.839
I am now logged onto a UNIX system at the
NCBI . I can do various things here.

00:15:47.839 --> 00:15:52.860
One of the things I could do is put one of
those eutilities URLs and do that.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:58.390
That is not particularly what you want to
have to do.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:04.079
You want to be able to use the programs esearch
and efetch to get things.

00:16:04.079 --> 00:16:07.060
Let's talk about that.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:10.899
Let's first reproduce the search that we had
in the slides a minute ago.

00:16:10.899 --> 00:16:15.920
Just to save myself some typing I will get
it out of this text file I have.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:17.129
It is on the ftp site.

00:16:17.129 --> 00:16:23.179
If you want to reproduce these yourself you
can.

00:16:23.179 --> 00:16:27.459
Let me put this in here first.

00:16:27.459 --> 00:16:34.410
I have changed the search slightly.

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:42.910
So that it is more complicated, it APRT in
the title, that's make sure I get just those

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:43.980
three sequences.

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:48.040
I don't want to deal with the human chromosome
sequence.

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:53.119
There is the Unix pipe.

00:16:53.119 --> 00:16:58.100
And I want the format Acc.

00:16:58.100 --> 00:17:04.689
So I have the three accession numbers I was
interested in before . I can use my up arrow

00:17:04.689 --> 00:17:08.370
key to get that back.

00:17:08.370 --> 00:17:14.040
And I can use get one of the other formats
I talked about FASTA . And if I want to, to

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:19.670
make it nicer, what will happen is it will
dump it to the screen so I can put it to a

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:21.080
file if I want to.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:28.060
Just call it APRT dot fna if I want to.

